-- title:
--   The crossing model modulo V is a domain
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in W$. Write $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi$ for the quotient of the ring of formal power series in two variables indexed by `Fin 2` over $W$ by the principal ideal generated by $X_0 X_1 - C(\pi)$, that is, for $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$, and let `V π` be the distinguished element of this ring (the second of the two crossing coordinates). Assume that the quotient $W/(\pi)$ by the ideal generated by $\pi$ is an integral domain, i.e. is nontrivial and has no nonzero zero divisors. The assertion is that the quotient of $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi$ by the ideal generated by `V π` is again an integral domain: it is nontrivial, and a product of two of its nonzero elements is nonzero. No hypothesis is placed on $W$ beyond commutativity, and none on $\pi$ beyond that the residue ring $W/(\pi)$ be a domain.
--
--   This is the statement that the local crossing model $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$ becomes, after killing one branch coordinate, the one-variable power series ring over $W/(\pi)$, hence a domain; for $W$ the Witt vectors of an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and $\pi = p$ it expresses that each branch of the ordinary double point on the special fibre of the $\Gamma_0(p)$-moduli problem at a supersingular point is integral. It is used to obtain that the ideal generated by `V π` is prime, in [`ModularCurve.UVCrossingModel.isPrime_span_V`](thm.html#ModularCurve.UVCrossingModel.isPrime_span_V).
